---
import Article from "../components/article.astro";
import Heading from "../components/heading.astro";
import Paragraph from "../components/paragraph.astro";
import { Popup } from "../components/popup";
import SectionLayout from "../layouts/section.astro";
---

<SectionLayout
  title="Conclusion"
  position="6"
  previousPath="/suppletion-and-lexemes"
>
  <Article page="conclusion" progress={0} previousPath="/suppletion-and-lexemes">
    <Heading>Congratulations</Heading>

    <Paragraph>
      You have reached the end of this web experience. You now know about some
      of the fundamental concepts of morphology, including morphemes, their
      basic classifications, and the phenomena of inflection, derivation,
      allomorphy, and suppletion!
    </Paragraph>

    <Paragraph>
      But the journey doesn’t end here! Morphology as a branch of linguistics is
      much, <b>much</b> larger than what we have covered on this website. We’ve discussed
      solutions to some of the easiest morphological problems that the English language
      presents, but indeed English can get much more complicated and irregular!
    </Paragraph>

    <Paragraph>
      It doesn’t stop at English either; the languages of the world have all
      sorts of different methods in which they form words, and you can learn
      more about these differences in the branch of <Popup
        client:idle
        triggerText="morphological typology"
        ><p>
          A branch of linguistics that classifies the languages of the world
          based on how they form words.
        </p></Popup
      >.
    </Paragraph>

    <Paragraph>
      You can continue experimenting with the English toolkit or your own
      conlang as you wish. Once you feel like your conlang has reached a state
      where you are ready to share it with the world, press the
      <b>&lt;TBD&gt;</b> button near the top corner to generate a shareable poster
      that helps anyone learn your conlang in 10 minutes! (Uses AI)
    </Paragraph>

    <Heading>Further reading</Heading>

    <Paragraph>
      If you wish to keep learning about different morphology concepts, here are
      some good resources:
    </Paragraph>

    <Paragraph className="font-medium text-teal-500">
      Lieber, R. (2016). <i>Introducing morphology</i> (Second edition.). Cambridge
      University Press.
    </Paragraph>

    <Paragraph className="ml-6">
      A concise and comprehensive introduction to morphology for students of
      linguistics. Covers a wide range of topics and serves as the primary
      source of information for this website.
    </Paragraph>

    <Paragraph className="font-medium text-teal-500">
      Quinion, M. (2026). <i>Affixes: The building blocks of English</i>.
      <a href="https://www.affixes.org/index.php">Affixes.Org</a>.
    </Paragraph>

    <Paragraph className="ml-6">
      A collection of affixes in the English language with their definitions and
      example usages. Useful if you would like to further explore the different
      affixes available in English.
    </Paragraph>

    <Paragraph className="font-medium text-teal-500">
      Ramoo, D. (2021). 3.3 Morphology of different languages.
      <i>Psychologyoflanguage.Pressbooks.Tru.Ca</i>.
      <a
        href="https://psychologyoflanguage.pressbooks.tru.ca/chapter/morphology-of-different-languages/"
        >https://psychologyoflanguage.pressbooks.tru.ca/chapter/morphology-of-different-languages/</a
      >
    </Paragraph>

    <Paragraph className="ml-6">
      This chapter from the online textbook “Psychology of Language” details the
      different ways in which languages can be classified based on their
      approach to word formation, and draws on examples from Turkish, Mandarin
      Chinese, Inuktitut, Arabic, and others. Check it out if you would like a
      quick intro to the morphologies of different languages of the world!
    </Paragraph>
  </Article>
</SectionLayout>
